<h2>Segment 312 &mdash; the limits of identical valid perceptions</h2>
<div class="pair">
<div class="tib"><div class="lab">TIBETAN (WYLIE)</div>
<span class="u" data-d="1" data-l="s312S"><span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c1">des na</span>, <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w1">las</span> can gyi <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w2">&#x27;gro ba</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w3">gsum</span> gyis <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w4">brlan</span> <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p1">zhing</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w5">gsher ba</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w6">phor pa</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w7">gang</span> la <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c2">cig car du</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w8">bltas pa</span> de&#x27;i tshe, <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w9">tshad</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w10">mtshungs</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w11">nges par</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w12">dgos pa</span> <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p2">ma</span> yin te, dgos <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p3">na</span>, <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w13">phor pa</span> gang po de&#x27;i <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c3">nang du</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w14">gnas</span> kyi blos <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w15">gnas</span> shing , <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w16">tha mal</span> gyi <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w17">mig shes</span> kyis mi bzod pa&#x27;i <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w18">srog chags</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w19">phra mo</span>&#x27;i <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w20">mig shes</span> kyis <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p4">kyang</span> , de&#x27;i tshad mar &#x27;gro <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w21">dgos pa</span> dang , <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w22">rgya mtsho</span>&#x27;i <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c4">nang na</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w23">gnas pa&#x27;i</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w24">srog chags</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w25">phra mo</span>&#x27;i <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w26">mig shes</span> kyis <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p5">kyang</span> , <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w27">rgya mtsho</span>&#x27;i <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w28">tshad</span> la <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w29">tshad mar</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w30">&#x27;gro</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w31">dgos pa</span> dang , <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w32">lha ma yin</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w33">&#x27;ga&#x27; zhig</span> la, <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w34">mtshon cha</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w35">phor</span> gang <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w36">snang</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w37">dgos pa</span> <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c5">la sogs pa&#x27;i</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w38">skyon</span> yod pa&#x27;i phyir</span>
</div>
<div class="eng"><div class="lab">GESHE MICHAEL'S ENGLISH (VERBATIM)</div>
<span class="u" data-d="1" data-l="s312S"><span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c1">In conclusion</span> now, let us consider again these <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w3">three</span> kinds of <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w2">beings</span>, each with their different <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w1">karma</span>, as they sit down <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c2">together</span> and <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w8">look</span> at a <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w6">glass</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w7">full</span> of something <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w4">wet</span> <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p1">and</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w5">flowing</span>. It&#x27;s <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p2">not</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w11">necessarily</span> true that they <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w12">must</span> all have <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w9">valid perceptions</span> which are <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w10">identical</span>. <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p3">If</span> they did, then the three beings looking at the <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w13">glass</span> of water would have to think of the water as a <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w14">place</span> to live, in the way that a creature <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w15">living</span> <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c3">in</span> water would. The three beings <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p4">as well</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w21">would have to</span> see the water in the same way that <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w19">microscopic</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w18">organisms</span> living in the water, little beings imperceptible to <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w16">normal</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w17">visual consciousness</span>, see it with their own <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w20">visual consciousness</span>. Then <span class="u" data-d="6" data-l="s312p5">too</span> the <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w26">visual consciousness</span> of <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w25">microscopic</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w24">organisms</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w23">living</span> <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c4">in</span> the depths of the <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w22">ocean</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w31">would have to</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w30">be</span> a <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w29">valid perception</span> towards the entire <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w28">extent</span> of the <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w27">sea</span>. And <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w33">certain</span> kinds of <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w32">near-gods</span> too <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w37">would have to</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w36">see</span> <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w34">weapons</span> as <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w35">glasses</span> of water, <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s312c5">and on and on</span>; the <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s312w38">problems</span> raised would be many.</span>
</div>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>AI NOTE</b> &mdash; Provisional machine alignment by Codex (English-first); GMR English remains verbatim and no hgm_gloss is changed. The three visual-consciousness occurrences distinguish normal vision, the glass organisms' own vision, and ocean organisms' vision. Opening tshad abbreviates valid perception; the later tshad means the sea's extent. The first tshad mar 'gro construction is distributed through GMR's expanded same-way comparison and remains unbanked; the ocean occurrence has the explicit valid perception exponent. mi bzod pa'i is also left unbanked: the local imperceptible rendering is contextually understandable but no separate lexical match is asserted. Repeated English participants, look/see predicates, water, and little are retained verbatim outside the selected spans.</div>
